                    round1_briefing:            `<strong>Metalicos del Valle S.A.S.</strong> (Cali, Colombia) manufactures structural steel products sold at <strong>COP 2,240/kg</strong>. Their largest client, <strong>Constructora Bogota Grande (CBG)</strong>, represents 31% of revenue (COP 26.35B/year, 11.76M kg/year) and is demanding a <strong>12% price reduction</strong>, citing Chinese steel available at COP 1,820/kg.<br><br>Your task: Calculate the <strong>REAL landed cost</strong> of Chinese steel in Bogota and the <strong>total effective price</strong> including all hidden costs. Also estimate the BATNA of Metalicos (revenue and EBITDA if CBG leaves).<br><br><strong>Chinese steel cost data:</strong><br>\u2022 FOB China: USD 520/ton (at COP 3,200/USD)<br>\u2022 Maritime freight Shanghai \u2192 Buenaventura: USD 42/ton<br>\u2022 Import tariff: 8% on CIF value<br>\u2022 Insurance and customs: COP 28/kg<br>\u2022 Transport Buenaventura \u2192 Bogota: COP 90/kg<br><br><strong>Hidden cost data:</strong><br>\u2022 Chinese lead time: 14 weeks (vs. 3 weeks for Metalicos)<br>\u2022 Additional inventory needed: 11 weeks of coverage<br>\u2022 CBG volume: 11.76M kg/year; cost of debt: 16% EA<br>\u2022 Quality rejection risk: 3-5% (est. COP 380M/year)<br>\u2022 Supply delay penalty: COP 280M/week (15% probability/year)<br><br><strong>BATNA data for Metalicos:</strong><br>\u2022 3 mid-size builders: COP 8.4B each, 2-4% lower prices, 6-month ramp-up<br>\u2022 Export to Ecuador/Peru: 96,000 ton at COP 2,180/kg, 4-month ramp`,
                    round2_briefing:            `Using the BATNAs from Round 1, now identify the <strong>Zone of Possible Agreement (ZOPA)</strong>.<br><br>The <strong>reservation price of Metalicos</strong> is the minimum price they would accept before preferring their BATNA. Consider that the BATNA has a 6-month transition risk and execution uncertainty.<br><br>The <strong>reservation price of CBG</strong> is the maximum they would pay before switching to Chinese steel. Consider the real TCO (not just the FOB price) plus the value CBG places on fast delivery (3 weeks vs. 14 weeks). The working capital cost of COP 816M/year for additional inventory equals COP 69/kg in delivery speed value.<br><br><strong>Key reference points:</strong><br>\u2022 Current price: COP 2,240/kg<br>\u2022 Real landed price of Chinese steel: COP 2,060/kg<br>\u2022 Effective price of Chinese steel (all costs): COP 2,165/kg<br>\u2022 BATNA EBITDA of Metalicos: COP 10.8B (slightly better than current COP 10.2B)<br>\u2022 BATNA has transition risk: 6-month ramp-up period`,
                    round3_briefing:            `The <strong>ZOPA is COP 2,080 \u2013 2,234/kg</strong> (width: COP 154/kg, or 6.9% of the base price). Now design the complete negotiation strategy for Metalicos.<br><br><strong>Strategy principles:</strong><br>\u2022 <strong>Anchor high with data:</strong> Open at or above COP 2,240/kg with value-added package<br>\u2022 <strong>Reveal TCO analysis:</strong> Show CBG that Chinese steel really costs COP 2,165/kg, not COP 1,820/kg<br>\u2022 <strong>Conditional concessions:</strong> Never concede without getting something in return<br>\u2022 <strong>Decreasing concession size:</strong> Each concession should be smaller than the previous one to signal approaching the limit<br>\u2022 <strong>Never reveal reservation price</strong><br><br><strong>Available concession levers:</strong><br>\u2022 Volume increase (CBG buys more kg/year)<br>\u2022 Contract length (2-3 years vs. current 1 year)<br>\u2022 Payment terms (currently 45 days)<br>\u2022 Quality certification (ICONTEC per lot)<br>\u2022 Delivery guarantee (3-week contractual commitment)<br>\u2022 Consignment inventory in Bogota`,
                    round4_briefing:            `Execute the final negotiation outcome. Based on your strategy, determine the terms of the agreement with CBG.<br><br><strong>Probable CBG tactics:</strong><br>\u2022 <strong>Aggressive anchoring:</strong> Insisting on the 12% reduction<br>\u2022 <strong>Exit threat:</strong> Claiming a pending Chinese steel shipment<br>\u2022 <strong>Issue separation:</strong> Trying to discuss quality/service before price<br>\u2022 <strong>False ultimatum:</strong> Accept 12% today or we sign with China tomorrow<br><br><strong>Your response toolkit:</strong><br>\u2022 Present TCO analysis before any counter-offer<br>\u2022 Test ultimatums: ask about specifics of the Chinese shipment<br>\u2022 Use issue expansion: bring volume, contract length, and payment terms to the table<br>\u2022 Apply interest-based negotiation: what does CBG really need beyond a lower price?<br><br><strong>Remember:</strong> A deal inside the ZOPA (COP 2,080 \u2013 2,234/kg) benefits both parties. Price above the midpoint (COP 2,157/kg) favors Metalicos.`,
                    r1_feedback_landed:         `Correct landed price:`,
